course: dictionary
lesson: 7375
title: Plonk: one word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one is meant. «His mate Bilal plonks his tray down next to him.» Here plonks means puts something down heavily or carelessly, because the thing being moved is a tray.
explain: In «Mrs Byrne plonks the whole jar on the whole loaf», plonks still means put down, but the careless, heavy way she does it is the funny part. Always look at the words around the word to find its meaning.
explain: Plonk can also be a noun, meaning cheap wine. Grown-ups use this word in British and Australian talk, so you may hear it at home. In a story, plonk can also be a sound, like one piano note dropping.
fact: «Plonk» as a verb means to put something down heavily or carelessly, as in «Plonk your bag there.»
fact: «Plonk» as a verb can also mean to sit down heavily, as in «He plonked himself on the sofa.»
fact: «Plonk» as a noun means cheap wine, and it is informal, so you would use it in relaxed talk rather than in a school report.
fact: «Plonk» as a noun can also be a dull, heavy sound, like a single piano note.
exercise: «His mate Bilal plonks his tray down next to him.» What does plonks mean here? | *Puts something down heavily or carelessly | Makes a sweet music note | Sits down slowly and gently | Drinks cheap wine
exercise: «He plonked himself on the sofa.» What is plonked doing here? | Singing a song loudly | Running out of the room | *Sitting down heavily | Carefully stacking cushions
exercise: Ella's dad says, «Fancy a glass of plonk?» What does plonk mean? | A sweet cake | *Cheap wine | A red fruit | A loud bang
test: «The coin went plonk into the pond.» What does plonk mean here? | Shiny and bright | Very slow and gentle | *A dull, heavy sound | A sweet taste
test: Mrs Byrne ___ the jar on the bread. Which word fits the gap? | Whispers | Tickles | Measures | *Plonks
exam: Which is the best way to check the meaning of a word like plonk? | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense | Choose the first option you see | Look at the word on its own and guess | Skip every sentence that has the word in it
